var tafsirData_30 = {
  "1": "\"Alif-Lam-Mim\" opens Surat al-Rum, the chapter of the Romans, with the same three letters that opened the spider chapter. Again their fullest meaning is with Allah. Their work is to mark what follows as Quran, then to drop the listener into a report about empires.\n\nThis surah will speak of a Byzantine defeat, a coming triumph within a few years, the promise of Allah, people who know only the surface of this life, travel among ruins, origin and resurrection, night and day praise, marriage, languages, sleep, lightning, and the split of people on the Hour. The letters are the threshold of that sweep from a battlefield to the last split.\n\nComing after \"We will guide them along Our Way,\" the letters remind that guidance includes believing news the unlettered Prophet could not have owned as a political analyst. The next words are not a proverb. They are a dated claim about Rome.",
  "2": "\"The Romans have been defeated\" is a headline from the world outside Mecca. \"Al-Rum\" are the Byzantines, the Christian empire that faced Persia. In the years around the Prophet's Meccan mission, Persian forces beat Rome badly, taking Jerusalem and much of the Near East. Pagans in Mecca, who felt closer to the Persian fire-worshippers than to the People of the Book, crowed. Believers who felt a nearer tie to those who had scripture were pained.\n\nThe verse states the defeat as fact, without yet explaining why it matters for tawhid. The next words will place it \"in a nearby land\" and promise a turn: following their defeat, they will triumph. The Quran does not deny the bad news. It frames it.\n\nA listener in 2026 still hears the method. Revelation can speak of real wars without becoming a war-manual. The defeat is a sign-post for a promise, and for the later sentence that the whole matter rests with Allah before and after victory.\n\nThe shortness of the verse matches a dispatch. The theology will grow in the verses that follow, not in extra adjectives here. Defeat is allowed to stand, so that the promised triumph will be felt as a true reversal, not as denial that Rome ever fell.",
  "3": "\"In a nearby land\" locates the defeat. \"Adna al-ard\" is the nearer part of the earth, from the Arabian vantage: the Levant, the lands of Syria and Palestine that sit closer than the far west of Rome. Commentators have long read it as the nearest edge of the Byzantine realm to the Arabs. The point is that this is not a rumor of a far myth. It is a war next door.\n\n\"Yet following their defeat, they will triumph.\" \"Min ba'di ghalabihim sa-yaghlibun\": after their being overcome, they will overcome. The Quran predicts a comeback. History records that Heraclius turned the war and beat Persia within the span the next verse names.\n\nThe believers' pain is being prepared for a joy. The pagans' mockery is being prepared for a silence. Neither army is being baptized as a flawless people of God. The next verses will say the believers will rejoice at a victory willed by Allah, who gives victory to whom He wills.\n\nNear land also means the sign is checkable. A false prophet inventing comfort would risk a date and a place. This Book takes that risk, then says the matter belongs to Allah before and after.",
  "4": "\"Within three to nine years\" is the English of \"fi bid'i sinin.\" \"Bid'\" in Arabic is a small number, used for a range from three to nine, or a few. The turn of Rome is placed inside that window, not in a vague someday.\n\n\"The whole matter rests with Allah before and after victory.\" \"Lillahi al-amr min qablu wa min ba'du.\" Defeat did not slip from His hand. Triumph will not. Before and after are His \"amr,\" His affair and command. Empires are not exceptions to tawhid.\n\n\"And on that day the believers will rejoice\" hangs into the next verse, which says they rejoice at the victory willed by Allah. The day of Rome's comeback becomes a day of believer's joy in Mecca, because the Quran's promise will have come true, and because a people of scripture will have risen against a people the pagans preferred.\n\nReports say Abu Bakr wagered with Meccan pagans on this prediction before betting was clearly forbidden, and that the turn came within the bid' years. Whether one stresses that report or the verse alone, the structure is: a numbered promise, Allah's ownership of the affair, and joy for believers.\n\nThe next verse completes the joy and names the Giver of victory as the Almighty, Most Merciful.",
  "5": "\"At the victory willed by Allah\" completes the believers' rejoicing. They do not rejoice as Byzantine nationalists. They rejoice \"bi-nasrillah,\" at Allah's help and victory. The English \"willed by\" captures that the nasr is His to give.\n\n\"He gives victory to whoever He wills.\" The same will that drowned Pharaoh and saved Abraham assigns battlefield outcomes. Rome's later win is not proof that every Christian doctrine is correct. It is proof that Allah turns affairs.\n\n\"For He is the Almighty, Most Merciful.\" \"Al-Aziz\" explains how a crushed army can rise. \"Al-Rahim\" explains why believers are given a sign that comforts them in a season of their own weakness in Mecca. Might and mercy meet in one reversal.\n\nA misconception is to read the verse as eternal divine partisanship for one empire. The verse says He gives victory to whom He wills. Another empire can fall later by the same will. The lesson for Quraysh is that the God of the Quran speaks truly about events they can watch.\n\nThe next verse names this report a promise of Allah, who never fails His promise, though most people do not know.",
  "6": "\"This is the promise of Allah. And Allah never fails in His promise.\" The Rome news is upgraded from analysis to \"wa'd,\" a promise. Failure, \"yukhlif,\" is denied of Allah. The same denial covers promises of resurrection, high rooms, and guidance for those who struggle.\n\n\"But most people do not know.\" They do not know that His promise does not fail, or they do not know the wider meaning of a war in the Levant. The next verse will say they only know the outward of this life.\n\nMost people in Mecca treated the Persian win as a final score and a taunt. Knowledge would have treated it as a scene inside a promise. When Rome later won, the knowers had a fulfilled wa'd in living memory.\n\nThe believer's joy, then, is joy in truthfulness, not only in a preferred team's recovery. The chapter will keep contrasting surface knowledge of dunya with blindness to the Hereafter. This promise sits at that border: a worldly war used as a lesson about a God who does not break His word.",
  "7": "\"They only know the worldly affairs of this life, but are totally oblivious to the Hereafter.\" \"Zahiran min al-hayat al-dunya,\" an outward of the worldly life. They are skilled in caravans, seasons, genealogies, and the news of Rome and Persia. That skill is real. It is also thin.\n\n\"Oblivious to the Hereafter\" is \"ghafilun,\" heedless. The Hour, the split of people, the return, do not sit in their daily knowing. So a promise about a war they understand is used to train them toward promises they ignore.\n\nThe verse is not a ban on knowing trade. It is a diagnosis of a knowledge that stops at the surface. Korah had a knowledge that gathered keys. These have a knowledge that reads battle maps. Neither is the knowledge of the spider parable.\n\nIf they knew the Hereafter as they know a market, they would hear \"Allah never fails His promise\" with trembling. The next verse asks whether they have not reflected upon their own being, and states that creation is for a purpose and a term, yet most deny the meeting with their Lord.",
  "8": "\"Have they not reflected upon their own being?\" \"Fi anfusihim,\" in themselves. The self is a book closer than the Levant. Body, birth, death, need, conscience—these are materials for thought. The question assumes they have not done this thinking with honesty.\n\n\"Allah only created the heavens and the earth and everything in between for a purpose and an appointed term.\" \"Bil-haqq\" and \"ajal musamma\": purpose and a named duration. The cosmos is not play in the sense of pointlessness, even if worldly life can become play for the heedless. It runs toward a term.\n\n\"Yet most people are truly in denial of the meeting with their Lord!\" \"Liqa' rabbihim,\" the same meeting this cluster of chapters has pressed. Most are \"kafirun\" with regard to it, covering it, refusing it. Reflection on the self should have made a meeting plausible: the One who began the self can receive it back.\n\nThe Rome promise has an appointed term of bid' years. Creation has an appointed term too. Learning to trust the short promise is training for the long one. The next verse sends them traveling to see the end of those who were stronger, who built more, whose messengers came, and who wronged themselves.",
  "9": "\"Have they not travelled throughout the land to see what was the end of those destroyed before them?\" The travel-command returns. Ends, \"aqibah,\" are visible. Meccan caravans already passed ruins.\n\n\"They were far superior in might; they cultivated the land and developed it more than these Meccans ever have.\" The destroyed were not primitive losers. They had \"quwwah,\" they tilled and built more than Quraysh, whose valley is not a great farm empire. So strength and development are not proofs of rightness.\n\n\"Their messengers came to them with clear proofs. Allah would have never wronged them, but it was they who wronged themselves.\" The law of the last chapters is restated. Bayyinat came. Zulm was their own. Allah is cleared.\n\nThe verse quietly compares Mecca to greater civilizations and still warns it. If more developed nations fell after messengers, a town of traders is not safe by being smaller. The next verse says the most evil end was that of the evildoers for denying and mocking the signs of Allah.",
  "10": "\"Then most evil was the end of the evildoers for denying and mocking the signs of Allah.\" \"Thumma kana 'aqibata alladhina asaoos-su'a,\" then the end of those who did evil was the worst evil. The cause is given: they denied the signs of Allah and took them in mockery, \"yastahzi'un.\"\n\nMockery was the tone of those who asked to hasten punishment, who called scriptures magic, who said bring the stones if you are true. This verse names that tone as the road to the worst aqibah.\n\nSigns include the recited Book, the ruins, the self, and the coming turn of Rome. Mocking any of that family is the evildoer's habit.\n\nThe English \"most evil\" keeps the moral quality of the end, not only its pain. It is a bad end because it answers a bad use of the signs. The next verse leaves history's ends for the cosmic pattern: Allah originates creation, will resurrect it, and then to Him you will be returned.",
  "11": "\"It is Allah Who originates the creation, and will resurrect it.\" \"Yabda'u\" and \"yu'iduhu\" again, the pair that makes resurrection a second work of the First Maker. The Rome cycle of defeat and triumph is a small cousin of this cycle: a fall, then a rising, under one Lord. The small cousin is not the same as the grave's rising, but it trains the mind to accept reversal by Allah.\n\n\"And then to Him you will all be returned.\" After origination and resurrection comes the return for judgment. Three steps, one Owner.\n\nPeople who only know the outward of this life watch births and deaths as weather. This verse gives the same events a direction: from Him, back to Him, then standing before Him.\n\nThe next verses open \"the Day the Hour will arrive\" with the wicked dumbstruck, with no intercessors from their associate-gods, and with a split of people into two groups. The return is given scenes.",
  "12": "\"On the Day the Hour will arrive, the wicked will be dumbstruck.\" \"Taqum al-sa'ah,\" the Hour stands up, arrives as a standing event. The \"mujrimun,\" the wicked, are \"yublis,\" struck with iblas: despairing silence, the look of someone whose last argument has died.\n\nDumbstruck matches earlier speechless scenes. The mouths that mocked signs, that hurried punishment, that said Allah created the heavens and then bowed to others, have no next sentence.\n\nThe Hour's arrival is certain in the same voice that dated Rome's return. One promise already checkable supports the promise that cannot be checked until it happens.\n\nThe next verse adds that they will have no intercessors from their associate-gods, and will deny those associates. Silence toward Allah and denial toward the partners is their state.",
  "13": "\"There will be no intercessors for them from among their associate-gods.\" \"Shufa'a'\" from among the partners they assigned to Allah will not appear as useful lawyers. The whole pagan system of \"these will intercede\" collapses.\n\n\"And they will totally deny their associate-gods.\" \"Yakfuruna bi-shuraka'ihim,\" they cover and reject the partners. The leaders who said it was not us they worshipped, and the followers who find the names silent, meet in this denial.\n\nThe verse is the court version of the ship: in the storm they dropped partners; on the Hour they drop them again, too late for sincerity to be a new religion of the shore.\n\nTaking protectors besides Allah was a spider's house. Here the house is not only weak. It is disowned by its own residents. The next verse says that on the Day the Hour arrives, people will be split into two groups.",
  "14": "\"And on the Day the Hour will arrive, the people will then be split into two groups.\" \"Yatafarraqun,\" they separate. The tests of the last chapter were already sorting truthful and liars. The Hour makes the sort visible as two companies.\n\nThe next two verses name them: those who believed and did good, rejoicing in a Garden; those who disbelieved and denied signs and the meeting, confined in punishment. The split is not by tribe, empire, or who won the Levant. Rome and Persia both dissolve into these two.\n\n\"The people\" is everyone the Hour gathers. No third safe island is mentioned here. That severity is the point of a chapter that began with news of two empires. Empires split; souls split more deeply.\n\nRejoicing was promised to believers on the day of Rome's victory. A greater rejoicing is about to be placed in a Garden. The rhyme teaches them not to stop at the smaller joy.",
  "15": "\"As for those who believed and did good, they will be rejoicing in a Garden.\" \"Yuhbarun,\" they are made glad, honored with joy, in a \"rawdah,\" a lush garden. Belief plus good work, the pair of these chapters, ends in a gladness that is not nervous.\n\nThe smaller joy at Allah's victory in the world was a taste. This is the meal. Both joys are gifts, not wages that exhaust the Giver.\n\nThe Garden as rawdah suggests ease, green, and rest after tests, travels, and the tasting of death. Elevated mansions in the last chapter and this rawdah are one home described from two angles.\n\nThe next verse is the other half of the split: denial of signs and of the meeting, and confinement in punishment. Joy and confinement are placed in matching frames so the choice now remains clear.",
  "16": "\"And as for those who disbelieved, and denied Our signs and the meeting with Allah in the Hereafter, they will be confined in punishment.\" Three marks: kufr, denial of ayat, denial of liqa' al-akhirah. The chapter has been naming those marks in Mecca's talk.\n\n\"Confined in punishment\" is \"muhdarun,\" brought to be present in the torment, held there. While the first group is spacious in a garden, this group is packed into penalty. The wide earth of \"My earth is spacious\" is gone for them.\n\nDenying the meeting was called the stance of most people a few verses ago. This is that stance at its end.\n\nThe following verses turn the living listener to praise: glorify Allah in the evening and morning, praise in the heavens and the earth, in the afternoon and at noon. After the split, worship is the sane response of those who still have time.",
  "17": "\"So glorify Allah in the evening and in the morning—\" \"Fa-subhan Allah\" when you come to evening, \"heena tumsun,\" and when you come to morning, \"heena tusbihun.\" The \"so\" ties tasbih to all that preceded: the promise, the Hour, the split. A person who believes that news fills the two edges of the day with glory to Allah.\n\nEvening and morning are the hinges of the sun that they admitted Allah subjected. Glorifying at those hinges is acting on their own confession.\n\nThe sentence continues into the next verse with praise in the heavens and the earth, afternoon and noon, so that the whole clock is mentioned. The break teaches that glorification is not one slot.\n\nCompanions took such verses as roots of the daily remembrances and of the prayers that sit at these times. The verse itself names the act: subhan, declaring Allah free of the partners and of failure to keep a promise.",
  "18": "\"All praise is for Him in the heavens and the earth—as well as in the afternoon, and at noon.\" Hamd fills the two realms. Then two more times: \"ashiyyan\" late day, and \"heena tuzhirun\" when you enter noon. Together with evening and morning, the day's quarters are claimed for praise.\n\nPraise \"in the heavens and the earth\" means the worlds already praise Him, and that praise is His right wherever the listener stands. Human tasbih joins a cosmic hamd.\n\nNoon, when the sun is highest, is a moment pagans might feel the sun's power. The verse plants praise of Allah in that heat. Afternoon, when markets tire, is planted too.\n\nThe next verse will give a reason to praise that returns to resurrection: He brings the living from the dead and the dead from the living, gives life to the earth after death, and so will you be brought forth. The clock of the day and the clock of the grave are one Lord's.",
  "19": "\"He brings forth the living from the dead and the dead from the living.\" A seed from a dry husk, a bird from an egg, a believer from a disbelieving house, a disbeliever from a believing home: the wording holds material and moral readings that scholars have both used. The shared point is Allah's reversal, the same art shown in Rome after defeat.\n\n\"And He gives life to the earth after its death.\" They already said Allah sends rain that does this. The verse takes their answer and aims it at the graves.\n\n\"And so will you be brought forth from the grave.\" \"Kadhālika tukhrajun.\" The \"so\" is the hinge of the argument. Earth-living after death is the analog. You will be brought out.\n\nThe chapter's opening reversal of an empire and this reversal of the dead are meant to be heard together. If Allah turns a war in bid' years, He can turn dust on a Day He has named. The next passage will list \"one of His signs\" after another, beginning with creation from dust, then marriage, then sleep and waking. The analog becomes a gallery of signs.",
  "20": "\"One of His signs is that He created you from dust, then—behold!—you are human beings spreading over the earth.\" The gallery of ayat begins with origin. \"Turab,\" dust, soil, the stuff of graves already, is the starting material. Then \"idha antum basharun tantashirun,\" suddenly you are humans spreading. The jump is the wonder. No idol sits at that jump.\n\nSpreading over the earth recalls the spacious earth of the last chapter and the travelers who were told to walk and see. Humanity's spread is itself a sign, not a proof that man is self-made.\n\nThe Rome story showed a nation falling and rising. This sign shows a species rising from dust and filling lands. Both are reversals under one Lord. The next sign will be closer to the chest: spouses from yourselves, comfort, compassion and mercy.",
  "21": "\"And one of His signs is that He created for you spouses from among yourselves so that you may find comfort in them.\" \"Azwaj\" from \"anfusikum,\" from yourselves, your own kind. The purpose is \"li-taskunu,\" to settle, to find sukun, rest. A home is meant to be a stillness after the spread over the earth.\n\n\"And He has placed between you compassion and mercy.\" \"Mawaddah\" and \"rahmah\" are placed, \"ja'ala,\" not merely evolved as a trick of appetite. Affection and mercy are installed gifts. Marriage in this verse is not only contract and desire. It is a sign that points beyond the couple to the One who placed the tenderness.\n\n\"Surely in this are signs for people who reflect.\" \"Yatafakkarun,\" they think. A harsh or purely commercial view of marriage misses the ayat. Reflection sees that comfort between two selves is as designed as the sun they admitted Allah subjected.\n\nThe next sign widens from the pair to the species: heavens and earth, and the diversity of languages and colors, for people of knowledge.",
  "22": "\"And one of His signs is the creation of the heavens and the earth, and the diversity of your languages and colours.\" The largest frame and a very human detail sit in one sentence. Skies and ground, then \"ikhtilaf alsinatikum wa alwanikum,\" the difference of your tongues and your colors.\n\nDiversity is not a mistake and not a license for pride. It is listed as a sign, like rain and spouses. People of \"al-alimin,\" sound knowledge, read many languages and many hues as one Author's variety, not as many gods or as a ladder of worth.\n\nMecca's markets already heard tongues. The verse trains the ear to hear in that noise a sign, not only a bargain.\n\nThe next sign is sleep by night and by day and seeking bounty, for people who listen. Knowledge, reflection, and listening are the rotating audiences of this gallery.",
  "23": "\"And one of His signs is your sleep by night and by day for rest as well as your seeking His bounty in both.\" Sleep can fall at night or as a nap in the day. Seeking fadl, provision, can happen in both too: night caravans, day markets. The pair of rest and seeking was assigned to night and day in the last chapter. Here both actions are allowed a share in both times, and both are signs.\n\n\"Surely in this are signs for people who listen.\" \"Yasma'un.\" Sleep is silent. The sign is heard by a heart that listens to the fact of losing consciousness and returning, a small death and a small rising every cycle.\n\nThe heedless who know only the outward of this life treat sleep as a gap in profit. Listeners treat it as an ayah of the One who will bring them forth. The next sign is lightning with hope and fear, and rain that revives the earth, for people who understand.",
  "24": "\"And one of His signs is that He shows you lightning, inspiring you with hope and fear.\" Lightning is one flash with two feelings: hope of rain, fear of strike. \"Khawfan wa tama'an\" in the wider Quranic pair appears here as hope and fear. The same cloud speaks two words.\n\n\"And He sends down rain from the sky, reviving the earth after its death.\" Their own answer, \"Allah,\" is now woven into the list of signs. Revival after death is repeated until the analog to graves cannot be honestly missed.\n\n\"Surely in this are signs for people who understand.\" \"Ya'qilun.\" Understanding ties flash, fear, hope, water, and green. The next sign is that the heavens and the earth persist by His command, and that one call will bring you forth from the earth instantly.",
  "25": "\"And one of His signs is that the heavens and the earth persist by His command.\" They \"taqum,\" stand and continue, \"bi-amrih.\" The sky does not fall and the ground does not fold because of a command that holds them, not because they are self-sufficient.\n\n\"Then when He calls you out of the earth just once, you will instantly come forth.\" \"Da'wah min al-ard,\" a single call, and \"idha antum takhrujun,\" you come out. The holding of the cosmos and the emptying of the graves are one authority. If He can hold the sky, He can call the dust.\n\nInstantly answers those who imagined resurrection as a long construction problem. The difficulty was only in their thought. The next verse says all in the heavens and the earth belong to Him and are subject to His will.",
  "26": "\"And to Him belong all those in the heavens and the earth—all are subject to His Will.\" \"Qanitun,\" devoutly obedient, submitted, subject. Angels, humans, jinn, creatures—the belonging is His, and the posture that fits belonging is qunut.\n\nSome are subject willingly, some despite themselves, as other verses distinguish. This line states the fact of subjection. Rome's armies and Mecca's chiefs are inside \"all those.\"\n\nThe verse blocks the idea of associate-gods who own a region of the sky or a tribe of the earth. Belonging is not shared. The next verse returns to originating and resurrecting, and says the resurrection is even easier for Him, and that the finest attributes belong to Him.",
  "27": "\"And He is the One Who originates the creation then will resurrect it—which is even easier for Him.\" \"Ahwana 'alayh,\" easier upon Him. For Allah, nothing is hard, as other verses say He is not wearied by creation. The wording is for our minds: if you admit the first making, the second is not the heavier lift.\n\n\"To Him belong the finest attributes in the heavens and the earth.\" \"Al-mathal al-a'la,\" the highest description, the finest attributes. No ugly trait of creation is to be thrown upon Him. Partners that eat, sleep, or are carved have no share in that high mathal.\n\n\"And He is the Almighty, All-Wise.\" The names that sealed Abraham's emigration and the spider verse seal this easier resurrection. The next verse sets an example from their own lives about not sharing wealth-partners from among those they possess, to shame the sharing of worship with Allah.",
  "28": "\"He sets forth for you an example drawn from your own lives: would you allow some of those bondspeople in your possession to be your equal partners in whatever wealth We have provided you, keeping them in mind as you are mindful of your peers?\" The parable uses a social fact they knew: a master does not make a slave an equal partner in his wealth, nor fear the slave's share as he fears a free peer's right. Yet they make created beings partners with the Creator in what is more than wealth: worship.\n\nThe example is aimed at their logic, not as an approval of every abuse of bondage. It takes their own pride of ownership and turns it against shirk. If you will not share your rizq as equals with those you claim to own, how do you share Allah's exclusive right with what He created?\n\n\"This is how We make the signs clear for people who understand.\" Clear signs include moral parables, not only lightning. The next verse says the wrongdoers merely follow desires with no knowledge, and who can guide those Allah has left to stray? They will have no helpers.",
  "29": "\"In fact, the wrongdoers merely follow their desires with no knowledge.\" After a clear parable, the remaining motive is \"ahwa',\" desires, without 'ilm. The last chapters said the same when they failed to bring a better book.\n\n\"Who then can guide those Allah has left to stray?\" When Allah leaves a person to stray after the signs, no caller owns a spare key. The Prophet was told he cannot guide whom he likes.\n\n\"They will have no helpers.\" \"Min nasirin.\" The associate-gods and the clans fail. The spider house is restated without the spider.\n\nThe next command is to set the face to the faith in uprightness, the natural way Allah instilled in people, with no change in Allah's creation, the Straight Way most people do not know.",
  "30": "\"So be steadfast in faith in all uprightness, O Prophet—the natural Way of Allah which He has instilled in all people.\" \"Aqim wajhaka lilddin hanifan,\" set your face to the religion, inclining away from shirk. That religion is \"fitrat Allah,\" the original nature Allah \"fatara\" people upon. Tawhid is not a foreign graft. It is the grain of the human.\n\n\"Let there be no change in this creation of Allah.\" Do not twist the fitrah, and do not think the created nature has been issued in a new edition that loves partners. Scholars have discussed whether this is a command or a statement that Allah's creation of the fitrah does not change. Both readings protect the original way.\n\n\"That is the Straight Way, but most people do not know.\" The straightness they lack is not a new geography. It is the nature they were given and then buried under custom.\n\nThe next verses tell believers to turn to Him, be mindful, establish prayers, and not be polytheists like those who split their religion into sects, each happy with what it has.",
  "31": "\"O believers! Always turn to Him in repentance, be mindful of Him, and establish prayers.\" Three practices that keep the face set: \"inibu,\" keep turning back; \"ittaquhu,\" keep taqwa; \"aqimu al-salah,\" keep prayer standing. Fitrah is preserved by habits, not only by a theory of nature.\n\n\"And do not be polytheists—\" The ban hangs into the next verse, which describes those who divided their faith. Shirk includes breaking the one religion into partisan toys.\n\nTurning, mindfulness, and prayer are also the opposite of following desires with no knowledge. They are how a person stays inside the instilled way when a town pulls toward partners.\n\nThe next verse completes the picture of what not to be: divided communities, each rejoicing in its own portion of the split.",
  "32": "\"Like those who have divided their faith and split into sects, each rejoicing in what they have.\" \"Farraqu dinahum wa kanoo shiya'an,\" they parted their religion and became parties. Each party \"farihun\" with what it has, glad of its fragment.\n\nThe joy is the danger. It is not grief at disunity but pride in a club, the mawaddah Abraham unmasked, harmony around a piece of the truth or around an idol.\n\nThe Hour will split people into two groups by faith and deeds. This worldly splitting into sects is a false preview, organized by desire.\n\nThe Prophet is told not to be of them. The community of the fitrah is one face set to one Lord. The next verses return to the storm-pattern: hardship makes people cry to their Lord alone; a taste of mercy, and a group associates again.",
  "33": "\"When people are touched with hardship, they cry out to their Lord, turning to Him alone.\" \"Mass al-nas durr,\" when harm touches. They \"da'aw rabbahum munibina ilayh,\" call their Lord, turning in inabah. The ship-scene is now a general human law.\n\n\"But as soon as He gives them a taste of His mercy, a group of them associates others with their Lord in worship.\" Not even all of them wait for the shore. A \"fariq,\" a group, returns to shirk at the first taste of rahmah. Mercy becomes the occasion of association, which is a bitter misuse.\n\nThe group is specified so that the verse does not claim every person apostasizes after relief. It claims a known pattern. The next verse names that pattern as ingratitude for favors, and again lets them enjoy, for soon they will know.",
  "34": "\"Becoming ungrateful for whatever favours We have given them.\" The associating group is defined by kufr of ni'mah, covering the favor. The taste of mercy is itself the favor they bury.\n\n\"So enjoy yourselves, for soon you will know.\" The same release as in the last chapter. Enjoyment is allowed as rope. Knowledge is postponed to a soon that will not be pleasant.\n\nThe verse is cold on purpose. After fitrah, prayer, and the ban on splitting religion, those who use mercy as fuel for shirk are not given another parable. They are given time.\n\nThe next question asks whether an authority has been sent down that attests to what they associate—whether they have a revealed warrant for partners.",
  "35": "\"Or have We sent down to them an authority which attests to what they associate with Him?\" \"Sultan,\" a warrant, a proof with authority, a revealed license. The question expects no. No kitab came from Allah saying these partners share His right.\n\nThey follow desires with no knowledge, and they have no sultan. The two lacks are the whole file of shirk's epistemology.\n\nThe challenge matches \"bring a scripture that guides better\" and \"show your proof.\" Association is not a second revelation. It is an addition without permission.\n\nThe next verse describes another swing of the soul: a taste of mercy makes them prideful; an evil they earned makes them despair. Pride and despair are two ways of missing the fitrah's balance.",
  "36": "\"If We give people a taste of mercy, they become prideful because of it.\" \"Farihu,\" they exult wrongly, the pride Korah was warned against. Mercy is read as entitlement or as proof of being chosen without obedience.\n\n\"But if they are afflicted with an evil for what their hands have done, they instantly fall into despair.\" The evil is tied to their hands. The response is \"yaqnatun,\" they despair. Hope for the meeting dies. Trust dies. They swing from swagger to collapse.\n\nThe believer between those poles has sabr and tawakkul. This verse is the diagnosis of the untrained soul that only knows the outward of this life: weather-vane religion.\n\nThe next verse asks whether they have not seen that Allah gives abundant or limited provision to whom He wills, and that in this are signs for people who believe. The swing is answered by watching His measure.",
  "37": "\"Have they not seen that Allah gives abundant or limited provisions to whoever He wills?\" The seeing is of a fact everyone meets: two neighbors, two lots. The will is His. Pride after wideness and despair after tightness both ignore that seeing.\n\n\"Surely in this are signs for people who believe.\" Believers read rizq as a sign, not only as income. The gallery of signs now includes the economy.\n\nGiving close relatives their due, and the poor and the traveler, will be the practical next step. If Allah widens and tightens, the one who is widened spends. The next verse commands that spending as best for those who seek Allah's pleasure, and they are the successful.",
  "38": "\"So give your close relatives their due, as well as the poor and the needy traveller.\" \"Ata dha al-qurba haqqahu,\" give the kin their right. Kin have a haqq, not only a plea. The poor and \"ibn al-sabil,\" the son of the road, share the command. Widened provision is channeled along these three.\n\n\"That is best for those who seek the pleasure of Allah, and it is they who will be successful.\" Seeking \"wajh Allah,\" His face and pleasure, makes the gift an act of the Hereafter. Success, falah, sits with them, not with those who hoard or who lend to grow by interest, as the next verse will say.\n\nThe verse turns signs into ethics. A person who glorifies morning and evening still has a purse to open. The next verse contrasts interest that does not increase with Allah and charity that is multiplied.",
  "39": "\"Whatever loans you give, only seeking interest at the expense of people's wealth will not increase with Allah.\" \"Riba\" is named by its aim: to increase on people's wealth. With Allah that increase does not grow. The books of the world may show a larger number. The scale of the Hereafter does not.\n\n\"But whatever charity you give, only seeking the pleasure of Allah—it is they whose reward will be multiplied.\" \"Zakat\" here is purifying charity, given for His face. Those givers, \"fa-ula'ika,\" are the ones who receive \"al-mud'ifun,\" multiplied payment.\n\nThe pair is sharp for a trading town. Mecca knew riba. The verse says their favorite growth is not growth, and the gift they fear will shrink them is what multiplies.\n\nSeeking interest at others' expense is a kind of istikbar in the market. Charity for His pleasure is fitrah in the purse. The next verses will return to creation, corruption people have earned, and the sending of messengers with clear proofs.",
  "40": "\"It is Allah Who created you, then gives you provisions, then will cause you to die, and then will bring you back to life.\" Four acts in order: create, feed, death, revival. No partner is invited into the sequence.\n\n\"Can any of your associate-gods do any of this?\" The question is clinical. Which partner creates, provides, takes life, and returns it? The expected answer is none.\n\n\"Glorified and Exalted is He above what they associate with Him in worship!\" Subhan and ta'ala lift Him above the associates after the four-act test. The gallery of signs has become a courtroom question.\n\nThe next verse names a result of human hands: corruption on land and sea, so they taste some of what they did, and perhaps they return.",
  "41": "\"Corruption has spread on land and sea as a result of what people's hands have done.\" \"Fasad\" in the two realms is tied to \"kasabat aydi al-nas,\" what hands earned. Droughts, wars, moral rot, ruined roads and seas—the verse allows a wide reading of corruption that humans cause, without forcing a modern slogan onto every storm.\n\n\"So that Allah may cause them to taste the consequences of some of their deeds and perhaps they might return to the Right Path.\" They taste \"ba'd,\" some, not all. The taste is pedagogical. \"La'allahum yarji'un,\" perhaps they will return. Even corruption's bitter fruit can be a mercy if it turns a person.\n\nThe verse does not say Allah is absent from the sea and land. It says human hands have a real causal role that He uses as a taste. The next command is again to travel and see the end of those before, most of whom were polytheists.",
  "42": "\"Say, O Prophet, 'Travel throughout the land and see what was the end of those destroyed before you—most of them were polytheists.'\" The ruins are given a majority diagnosis: \"kana aktharuhum mushrikin.\" Shirk is not a small side-sin in fallen nations. It is the common air they breathed.\n\nTravel plus seeing plus this label connects fasad on land and sea to association with Allah. Peoples who split the Creator's right did not last as they hoped.\n\nMost, not all, were polytheists. The verse is careful. The lesson remains: the end of mushrik nations is a visible tafsir of the four acts only Allah does.\n\nThe next verse tells the Prophet to set himself to the upright faith before a Day from Allah that cannot be turned back, when people will be divided.",
  "43": "\"So be steadfast in the Upright Faith, O Prophet, before the coming of a Day from Allah that cannot be averted.\" \"Aqim wajhaka lilddin al-qayyim,\" the same setting of the face, now timed: before a Day that \"la maradda lahu,\" has no turning back, from Allah.\n\n\"On that Day the people will be divided.\" The split already named returns as the reason to be steadfast now. After that Day, changing course is over.\n\nThe Prophet is addressed, and his community follows. Upright faith is the fitrah-religion before the lock of the Hour. The next verse assigns the two sides: disbelievers bear their disbelief; those who did good have prepared for themselves.",
  "44": "\"Those who disbelieved will bear the burden of their own disbelief.\" \"Man kafara fa-'alayhi kufruh.\" The load is his. The offer to carry someone else's sins is dead.\n\n\"And those who did good will have prepared for themselves eternal homes.\" \"Man 'amila salihan fa-li-anfusihim yamhadun,\" they make a cradle, a prepared place, for their own souls. Good work is furnishing a future room.\n\nThe division is personal. Empire, clan, and sect do not absorb the burden or the preparation. The next verse states the purpose: that He may reward those who believe and do good out of His grace, and that He does not like the disbelievers.",
  "45": "\"So that He may generously reward those who believe and do good, out of His grace.\" The preparation of the doers is met by \"yajziya,\" reward, \"min fadlih,\" from His bounty. Even prepared homes are entered by grace, not by putting Allah in debt.\n\n\"He truly does not like the disbelievers.\" \"La yuhibbu al-kafirin.\" Love here is not a mood. It is His not being pleased with kufr as a stance. The statement is plain after so many invitations.\n\nThe next sign returns to kindness in this world: winds ushering good news of rain, a taste of mercy, ships sailing, seeking bounty, perhaps you will be grateful.",
  "46": "\"And one of His signs is that He sends the winds, ushering in good news of rain so that He may give you a taste of His mercy, and that ships may sail by His command, and that you may seek His bounty, and perhaps you will be grateful.\" Winds are a sign with several fruits: bushra of rain, a taste of rahmah, ships that move \"bi-amrih,\" seeking fadl, and the aim of shukr.\n\nThe ship that terrified them into sincere du'a is here a mercy when it sails by command. Same sea, two lessons: storm-tawhid and fair-wind gratitude.\n\nPerhaps you will be grateful gathers the whole gallery. Signs are not for collection. They are for thanks. The next verse recalls messengers to their own peoples, punishment of the wicked, and that it is Allah's duty to help the believers.",
  "47": "\"Indeed, We sent before you, O Prophet, messengers, each to their own people, and they came to them with clear proofs.\" He is not the first. Each rasul went to \"ummatihi,\" his people, with bayyinat.\n\n\"Then We inflicted punishment upon those who persisted in wickedness.\" \"Alladhina ajramu,\" those who persisted in crime. Punishment follows persistence, the old law.\n\n\"For it is Our duty to help the believers.\" \"Haqqan 'alayna nasr al-mu'minin,\" a right We have taken upon Ourselves, to help the believers. The Rome victory and every smaller relief sit under this haqq. It does not always mean immediate battlefield win. It means Allah has obligated Himself, in His wisdom, to nasr.\n\nThe next verses linger on winds, clouds, rain, rejoicing after despair, then the impact of mercy that can raise the dead.",
  "48": "\"It is Allah Who sends the winds, which then stir up vapour, forming clouds, which He then spreads out in the sky or piles up into masses as He wills, from which you see rain come forth.\" The process is walked step by step so that habit does not hide the Actor: winds, clouds, spreading or piling \"kayfa yasha',\" as He wills, then rain you see.\n\n\"Then as soon as He causes it to fall on whoever He wills of His servants, they rejoice.\" The fall is targeted, \"man yasha'u min 'ibadih.\" Rejoicing is human and allowed. The next verse will say they had lost hope just before.\n\nSeeing rain come forth is the opposite of heedlessness. The gallery asks the eye to stay awake in weather. The despair-then-joy of farmers is about to become another sign of how souls swing.",
  "49": "\"Although they had utterly lost hope just before it was sent down to them.\" \"Min qabli an yunazzala 'alayhim la-mublisin,\" they were in despairing silence before the sending down. The same iblas that will strike the wicked on the Hour can strike a dry field.\n\nRain after iblas is a small resurrection of mood. The verse exposes how quickly humans move from \"nothing will come\" to cheers, without always moving to thanks.\n\nThe next command is to see the impact of Allah's mercy: how He gives life to the earth after death, and that the same Creator can raise the dead, for He is capable of everything.",
  "50": "\"See then the impact of Allah's mercy: how He gives life to the earth after its death!\" \"Athar rahmat Allah,\" the trace and impact of mercy, is green after brown. See, the old verb of these chapters.\n\n\"Surely That same Creator can raise the dead. For He is Most Capable of everything.\" The analog is now a verdict. Capability, \"qadir,\" seals it.\n\nA person who has watched a dead valley live has already attended a rehearsal. Refusing the rehearsal's meaning is the heedlessness of those who only know the outward.\n\nThe next verse shows the opposite weather: a harsh wind that withers crops, and they deny old favours at once. Joy-theology that cannot survive a scorching wind is not faith.",
  "51": "\"Then if We send a harsh wind which they see withering their crops, they will definitely deny old favours right after.\" They see the yellowing, \"musfarran,\" and \"la-zallu min ba'dihi yakfurun,\" they go on in kufr, covering the past favors. One bad season erases rain-memory.\n\nThis is the pride-and-despair swing in agriculture. Gratitude that lasts only while the field is green is the shore-shirk of farmers.\n\nThe Prophet is about to be told again that he cannot make the dead hear, nor the deaf, nor lead the blind. A withered field that produces denial is a picture of those unhearing souls.",
  "52": "\"So you, O Prophet, certainly cannot make the dead hear the truth. Nor can you make the deaf hear the call when they turn their backs and walk away.\" The refrain from the chapter of the ant returns after the weather signs. Dead and deaf, plus turning backs, are three barriers. The signs of rain and dust and spouses do not force hearing.\n\nThe \"so\" links their kufr after a scorching wind to his limited power. He is not blamed. He is bounded.\n\nThe next verse adds the blind and the exception: only those who believe in the revelations and submit will hear.",
  "53": "\"Nor can you lead the blind out of their misguidance. You can make none hear the truth except those who believe in Our revelations, fully submitting to Allah.\" The third picture, then the exception \"muslimun.\" Hearing is for believers who yield.\n\nThe gallery of signs was public. The hearing is not. That is why most people do not know, and why tests distinguish.\n\nThe next sign is the human life-cycle: weakness, strength, weakness and old age. He creates what He wills, All-Knowing, Most Capable. The body itself preaches what the dead, deaf, and blind will not hear.",
  "54": "\"It is Allah Who created you in a state of weakness, then developed your weakness into strength, then developed your strength into weakness and old age.\" Three stages: \"da'f,\" then \"quwwah,\" then da'f and \"shaybah,\" white hair. Rome's defeat and triumph are written also on a single back.\n\n\"He creates whatever He wills. For He is the All-Knowing, Most Capable.\" The stages are created, not accidents. Knowledge and capability own the aging of a king and of a poor man.\n\nThe one who is proud in the middle stage is ignoring the two weaknesses that frame him. The next verse jumps to the Hour, when the wicked will swear they stayed in the world no more than an hour, and that this is how they were always deluded.",
  "55": "\"And on the Day the Hour will arrive, the wicked will swear that they did not stay in this world more than an hour.\" The long life of strength will shrink in memory to \"sa'ah,\" an hour. Time on that Day revalues time here.\n\n\"In this way they were always deluded in the world.\" \"Kadhalika kanoo yu'fakun.\" The same turning-away that made them misread rain and ruins makes them misread duration. Delusion about time is part of delusion about meeting Allah.\n\nThe next verse lets those gifted with knowledge and faith correct them: you stayed as Allah destined until the Day of Resurrection, and here it is, but you did not know.",
  "56": "\"But those gifted with knowledge and faith will say to them, 'You did actually stay—as destined by Allah—until the Day of Resurrection.'\" Knowledge and iman speak together, as they did against Korah's parade. The stay was \"fi kitabi Allah,\" in Allah's decree and writing, until \"yawm al-ba'th.\"\n\n\"So here is the Day of Resurrection which you denied! But you did not know it was true.\" The Day is pointed to as present. Their not-knowing was not lack of warning. It was refusal.\n\nThe contrast is beautiful: the wicked guess an hour; the knowers recall a destined span and name the Day. The next verse says the wrongdoers' excuses will not benefit, nor will they be allowed to make amends.",
  "57": "\"So on that Day the wrongdoers' excuses will not benefit them, nor will they be allowed to appease their Lord.\" \"La yanfa'u alladhina zalamu ma'dhiratuhum,\" their excuses do not profit. \"Wa la hum yusta'tabun,\" they are not given a chance to seek 'utba, to please and make up.\n\nThe door of return that corruption's taste was meant to open is closed then. Appeasement is for the spacious earth and the years of weakness and strength.\n\nThe next verse says every kind of lesson has been set in this Quran, yet whatever sign the Prophet brings, the disbelievers will say to the believers, you are only people of falsehood.",
  "58": "\"We have certainly set forth every kind of lesson for people in this Quran.\" \"Min kulli mathal,\" every sort of example and lesson. Empires, ruins, spouses, colors, rain, aging, riba, fitrah—the claim is that the Book is not short of illustrations.\n\n\"And no matter what sign you bring to them, O Prophet, the disbelievers will definitely say to the believers, 'You are only a people of falsehood.'\" The response is locked: \"mubtilun,\" people of batil. Signs change; the stamp does not.\n\nThis prepares the next line: Allah seals the hearts of those unwilling to know. The problem is not a missing mathal.",
  "59": "\"This is how Allah seals the hearts of those unwilling to know the truth.\" \"Yatba'u Allahu 'ala qulubi alladhina la ya'lamun.\" They do not know because they will not know. The seal follows that unwillingness, as other verses seal after arrogance and mockery.\n\nThe Prophet who cannot make the dead hear is shown why: a seal on hearts that refused knowledge. The Rome promise, the gallery of signs, the lessons of every kind, meet a locked door.\n\nThe last verse of the chapter will tell him to be patient, for the promise of Allah is true, and not to be disturbed by those who have no sure faith. Patience is the stance toward sealed hearts and a true wa'd.",
  "60": "\"So be patient, for the promise of Allah certainly is true.\" The chapter that opened with a dated promise about Rome closes by telling the Prophet \"isbir.\" The reason is the same wa'd: Allah's promise is true. Rome's turn, the Hour, help for believers, multiplied charity—all sit in that truth.\n\n\"And do not be disturbed by those who have no sure faith.\" \"La yastakhiffannaka,\" let them not shake you, lighten you, pull you off your weight, those who have no \"yaqin.\" The last chapter praised sure faith in the Hereafter. People without it are noisy. Their noise is not a measure of the promise.\n\nPatience here is not sitting still only. It is remaining on the upright faith until the Day that cannot be averted, while signs are mocked as falsehood. The next surah will open with letters and a wise Book that is a guide and mercy for good-doers who pray, give, and have that same sure faith."
};
